// Rep Max questions: Chemistry (AQA 8462), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gch-001","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which technique is used to get pure water from salt solution?","o":["Filtration","Paper chromatography","Simple distillation","Crystallisation"],"a":2,"x":"Distillation evaporates the water and condenses it, leaving the salt behind. Crystallisation keeps the salt, and filtration only removes insoluble solids."}
{"id":"gch-002","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.3","lv":1,"q":"A chlorine atom has atomic number 17 and mass number 37. How many neutrons does it have?","o":["17","37","20","54"],"a":2,"x":"Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 37 − 17 = 20. The atomic number is the number of protons, which is also 17."}
{"id":"gch-003","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.1.7","lv":1,"q":"Phosphorus has atomic number 15. What is its electronic structure?","o":["2,8,8,5","2,5,8","2,8,5","2,13"],"a":2,"x":"15 electrons fill the shells in order: 2 in the first, 8 in the second, leaving 5 in the third shell. The structure is 2,8,5."}
{"id":"gch-004","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Why did Mendeleev leave gaps in his periodic table?","o":["He did not have data on the first few elements","He ordered the elements by atomic number, which left holes","The gaps were for isotopes of the elements nearby","The gaps were for elements that had not been discovered yet"],"a":3,"x":"Mendeleev switched the order of some elements to fit patterns in properties and left gaps, predicting undiscovered elements. Later discoveries matched his predictions."}
{"id":"gch-005","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Rubidium is more reactive than lithium. What is the reason?","o":["It has more electrons in its outer shell","Its outer electron is held more strongly by the nucleus","It has a higher melting point than lithium","Its outer electron is further from the nucleus"],"a":3,"x":"Down Group 1 the outer electron is further from the nucleus and more shielded, so the attraction is weaker and the electron is lost more easily."}
{"id":"gch-006","s":"chemistry","t":"4.1.3","lv":1,"q":"Compared with Group 1 metals, transition metals are generally...","o":["softer, less dense and more reactive","harder, denser and more reactive","softer, denser and less reactive","harder, denser and less reactive"],"a":3,"x":"Transition metals have high melting points, high densities and are hard and strong, and they react less vigorously than Group 1. Group 1 metals are soft and very reactive."}
{"id":"gch-007","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"Aluminium forms Al³⁺ ions and oxygen forms O²⁻ ions. What is the formula of aluminium oxide?","o":["Al₂O₃","AlO","Al₃O₂","Al₂O₂"],"a":0,"x":"The charges must cancel. Two Al³⁺ give +6, and three O²⁻ give −6, so the ratio of ions is 2 : 3 and the formula is Al₂O₃."}
{"id":"gch-008","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.1","lv":1,"q":"In a chemical equation, what does the state symbol (aq) mean?","o":["Dissolved in water","In the gas state","In the solid state","As a pure liquid"],"a":0,"x":"(aq) means aqueous: the substance is dissolved in water. The other symbols are (s) solid, (l) liquid and (g) gas."}
{"id":"gch-009","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does solid sodium chloride not conduct electricity, but molten sodium chloride does?","o":["In the molten state electrons are free to move","Molten sodium chloride contains small molecules","In the molten state the ions are free to move","Solid sodium chloride has no charged particles"],"a":2,"x":"In a solid the ions are held in fixed positions in the lattice. When melted the ions can move and carry charge, so the liquid conducts."}
{"id":"gch-010","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.3","lv":2,"q":"Why does graphite conduct electricity?","o":["One electron per carbon atom is delocalised and can move","It contains positive ions that are free to move through it","It is a metal, so it has a sea of free electrons","Each carbon forms four bonds, leaving no free electrons"],"a":0,"x":"In graphite each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds, leaving one delocalised electron per atom. These electrons move along the layers and carry charge."}
{"id":"gch-011","s":"chemistry","t":"4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"Particle A is a cube of side 5 nm and particle B is a cube of side 50 nm. How do their surface area to volume ratios compare?","o":["B's ratio is 10 times larger than A's","Their ratios are the same","A's ratio is 10 times larger than B's","A's ratio is 100 times larger than B's"],"a":2,"x":"For a cube the ratio is 6 ÷ side length. A: 6 ÷ 5 = 1.2 and B: 6 ÷ 50 = 0.12, so A's is 10 times larger. Nanoparticles have a huge ratio."}
{"id":"gch-012","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.1","lv":2,"q":"Propane burns: C₃H₈ + ___O₂ → 3CO₂ + 4H₂O. What number goes in front of O₂ to balance the equation?","o":["3","5","4","10"],"a":1,"x":"The right side has 3 × 2 + 4 = 10 oxygen atoms. Each O₂ molecule has two atoms, so 10 ÷ 2 = 5 molecules of O₂ are needed."}
{"id":"gch-013","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.2","lv":2,"q":"What is the percentage by mass of nitrogen in ammonium nitrate, NH₄NO₃? (Ar: N = 14, H = 1, O = 16)","o":["17.5%","35.0%","28.0%","70.0%"],"a":1,"x":"Mr = 14 + 4 + 14 + 48 = 80. There are two nitrogen atoms, 28 in total. Percentage = 28 ÷ 80 × 100 = 35.0%.","w":["Used only one nitrogen atom: 14 ÷ 80, but the formula has two N.","","Used the mass 28 as if it were the percentage; you must divide by 80.","Used 28 for each N (like N₂); each N atom is 14, so 28 ÷ 80 = 35%."]}
{"id":"gch-014","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.1.4","lv":2,"q":"Three titration volumes were 24.10, 24.30 and 24.20 cm³. Taking the uncertainty as half the range, what are the mean and the uncertainty?","o":["24.20 ± 0.20 cm³","24.20 ± 0.10 cm³","24.30 ± 0.10 cm³","24.20 ± 0.05 cm³"],"a":1,"x":"Mean = 72.60 ÷ 3 = 24.20 cm³. The range is 0.20, and the uncertainty is half the range: ± 0.10 cm³.","w":["Used the whole range as the uncertainty; it should be half the range.","","Mean is wrong: 24.30 is the largest value, not the mean of the three.","Halved the range twice; half of 0.20 is 0.10, not 0.05."]}
{"id":"gch-015","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.5","lv":2,"q":"5.0 g of sodium hydroxide is dissolved in water to make 250 cm³ of solution. What is the concentration in g/dm³?","o":["1.25 g/dm³","20 g/dm³","1250 g/dm³","0.02 g/dm³"],"a":1,"x":"Convert 250 cm³ to 0.25 dm³. Concentration = mass ÷ volume = 5.0 ÷ 0.25 = 20 g/dm³.","w":["Multiplied by 0.25 instead of dividing; dividing by 0.25 gives a larger number.","","Multiplied 5.0 by 250; divide by the volume in dm³ (0.25) instead.","Divided by 250 without converting to dm³, so the answer is far too small."]}
{"id":"gch-016","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.3","lv":3,"q":"Hydrogen is made by CH₄ + H₂O → CO + 3H₂. Mr: CH₄ = 16, H₂O = 18, H₂ = 2. What is the atom economy for making hydrogen?","o":["17.6%","5.9%","33.3%","37.5%"],"a":0,"x":"Atom economy = Mr of desired product ÷ Mr of all reactants × 100 = (3 × 2) ÷ (16 + 18) × 100 = 6 ÷ 34 × 100 = 17.6%.","w":["","Used only one H₂ (2) instead of 3H₂ (6); the equation has 3 moles of H₂.","Divided by the Mr of water only; use the total Mr of both reactants.","Divided by the Mr of methane only; use the total Mr of both reactants."]}
{"id":"gch-017","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which metal will not displace copper from copper sulfate solution?","o":["Zinc","Iron","Magnesium","Silver"],"a":3,"x":"A more reactive metal displaces a less reactive one. Silver is below copper in the reactivity series, so there is no reaction.","w":["Zinc is more reactive than copper, so it does displace it.","Iron is more reactive than copper, so it does displace it.","Magnesium is much more reactive than copper, so it does displace it.",""]}
{"id":"gch-018","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A student makes copper sulfate crystals by adding copper oxide to warm sulfuric acid. Why is the copper oxide added in excess?","o":["To make the reaction go faster","To make sure all the acid has reacted","To make the solution acidic at the end","To make the crystals larger"],"a":1,"x":"Copper oxide is insoluble, so the extra solid is simply filtered off, leaving a neutral salt solution with no acid left. Then it is evaporated and crystallised.","w":["Speed isn't the aim; extra oxide makes sure no acid is left over.","","Excess oxide removes the acid, so the solution ends neutral, not acidic.","Crystal size depends on how slowly it crystallises, not on excess oxide."]}
{"id":"gch-019","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.2.4","lv":2,"q":"In a titration, acid from a burette is added to alkali in a flask containing phenolphthalein. What shows the end-point?","o":["Colourless to pink","Blue to green","Pink to colourless","Red to orange"],"a":2,"x":"Phenolphthalein is pink in alkali and colourless in acid. When the alkali is just neutralised the pink colour disappears.","w":["This is the reverse: it fits alkali being added to acid, not acid to alkali.","Phenolphthalein is not blue or green in any solution; those aren't its colours.","","Red to orange is not a phenolphthalein change; it is pink in alkali, colourless in acid."]}
{"id":"gch-020","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.1","lv":2,"q":"Why is aluminium oxide dissolved in molten cryolite for electrolysis?","o":["To make the aluminium that forms harder","To react with the oxygen that forms at the anode","To stop the carbon anodes from burning away","To lower the melting point and save energy"],"a":3,"x":"Aluminium oxide melts at over 2000 °C. Dissolving it in molten cryolite lowers the temperature needed, which saves energy and cost.","w":["Cryolite does not affect the metal's hardness; it affects the melting temperature.","Oxygen reacts with the carbon anodes, not the cryolite.","The anodes still burn away and need replacing; cryolite doesn't prevent this.",""]}
{"id":"gch-021","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.4","lv":3,"q":"Copper sulfate solution is electrolysed with inert electrodes. What forms at each electrode?","o":["Copper at the cathode and oxygen at the anode","Hydrogen at the cathode and oxygen at the anode","Copper at the cathode and hydrogen at the anode","Hydrogen at the cathode and sulfur at the anode"],"a":0,"x":"Copper is less reactive than hydrogen, so copper forms at the cathode. Sulfate ions are not discharged, so hydroxide ions lose electrons to give oxygen at the anode.","w":["","Hydrogen would only form at the cathode if the metal were more reactive than hydrogen.","Hydrogen ions are positive, so they go to the cathode, never the anode.","Sulfur is not formed; sulfate ions stay in solution and hydroxide gives oxygen."]}
{"id":"gch-022","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.1","lv":1,"q":"On a reaction profile the products are at a lower energy than the reactants. What does this show?","o":["The reaction is endothermic and takes in energy","The reaction can only happen with a catalyst","The reaction needs no activation energy","The reaction is exothermic and gives out energy"],"a":3,"x":"If the products have less energy than the reactants, the difference is transferred to the surroundings, so the temperature rises. This is exothermic.","w":["Mixed up the terms: endothermic would have products at higher energy.","Catalysts only lower the activation energy; they don't decide the energy change.","Every reaction needs activation energy to get started, even exothermic ones.",""]}
{"id":"gch-023","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.2","lv":1,"q":"What is the only product of a hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell?","o":["Carbon dioxide","Ammonia","Hydrogen peroxide","Water"],"a":3,"x":"Hydrogen is oxidised in the cell: 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. The only product is water, so no carbon dioxide is released.","w":["CO₂ comes from burning fossil fuels; this cell has no carbon in it.","There is no nitrogen in the reactants, so ammonia cannot form.","Peroxide has extra oxygen; the cell's overall reaction gives H₂O only.",""]}
{"id":"gch-024","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A gas syringe shows 24 cm³ at 20 s and 44 cm³ at 60 s. What is the mean rate between 20 s and 60 s?","o":["0.73 cm³/s","1.2 cm³/s","2.0 cm³/s","0.5 cm³/s"],"a":3,"x":"Mean rate = change in volume ÷ change in time = (44 − 24) ÷ (60 − 20) = 20 ÷ 40 = 0.5 cm³/s.","w":["Divided the final volume 44 by 60 s; you need the change in both volume and time.","Used 24 ÷ 20, the rate for the first 20 s only, not 20 s to 60 s.","Divided time by volume (40 ÷ 20) the wrong way round; rate is volume ÷ time.",""]}
{"id":"gch-025","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Why does raising the temperature make a reaction faster?","o":["Particles collide more often and with more energy","It lowers the activation energy of the reaction","There are more particles in the same volume","The particles collide less often but with more energy"],"a":0,"x":"Hotter particles move faster, so collisions are more frequent. A greater proportion also have at least the activation energy, so more collisions are successful.","w":["","Mixes this up with a catalyst; heating does not lower the activation energy.","That is concentration or pressure; heating adds no extra particles to the volume.","Hotter particles move faster, so they collide more often, not less."]}
{"id":"gch-026","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.1","lv":2,"q":"What is true about a reversible reaction at dynamic equilibrium in a closed system?","o":["The forward and reverse reactions happen at the same rate","Both reactions have stopped","The concentrations of reactants and products are equal","Only the forward reaction is still happening"],"a":0,"x":"At equilibrium both reactions continue at equal rates, so the concentrations stay constant. They are not necessarily equal.","w":["","Equilibrium is dynamic: both reactions carry on, just at equal rates.","Equal rates do not mean equal concentrations; they just stay constant.","In a reversible reaction the reverse reaction carries on too."]}
{"id":"gch-027","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Compared with a fraction made of long hydrocarbon chains, a fraction of short chains has...","o":["a higher boiling point and is more flammable","a lower boiling point and is more viscous","a higher boiling point and is more viscous","a lower boiling point and is more flammable"],"a":3,"x":"Shorter chains have weaker forces between molecules, so they boil at lower temperatures, flow easily (low viscosity) and ignite more easily.","w":["Short chains have weaker forces between molecules, so a lower boiling point, not higher.","Lower boiling point is right, but short chains are less viscous, not more.","Both wrong: short chains boil at lower temperatures and flow more easily.",""]}
{"id":"gch-028","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.1.4","lv":1,"q":"What happens when an alkene is shaken with bromine water?","o":["The bromine water changes from colourless to orange","The bromine water changes from orange to colourless","The bromine water stays orange","The bromine water turns blue"],"a":1,"x":"Alkenes have a C=C double bond. Bromine adds across it, so the orange bromine water is decolourised. Alkanes do not react.","w":["Colours reversed: bromine water starts orange and ends colourless.","","That is what alkanes do; alkenes react with bromine and decolourise it.","Bromine water never turns blue; alkenes decolourise it."]}
{"id":"gch-029","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.1","lv":1,"q":"What is made when ethene reacts with steam in the presence of a catalyst?","o":["Ethane","Ethanoic acid","Poly(ethene)","Ethanol"],"a":3,"x":"Steam adds across the double bond: C₂H₄ + H₂O → C₂H₅OH. This is one way to make ethanol industrially.","w":["Ethane would need hydrogen added, not steam.","Ethanoic acid needs oxidation; steam just adds H and OH across the C=C.","Poly(ethene) forms when many ethene molecules join, not by reacting with steam.",""]}
{"id":"gch-030","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.2.3","lv":2,"q":"What is the formula of propanoic acid?","o":["CH₃CH₂COOH","CH₃CH₂CH₂OH","CH₃COOH","CH₃CH₂CHO"],"a":0,"x":"Carboxylic acids contain –COOH. Propanoic acid has three carbon atoms, so it is CH₃CH₂COOH. CH₃COOH is ethanoic acid.","w":["","That is propanol (an alcohol, –OH); propanoic acid needs –COOH.","That is ethanoic acid with two carbons; propanoic acid has three.","That is propanal (an aldehyde, –CHO), not a carboxylic acid."]}
{"id":"gch-031","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which molecule can act as a monomer in addition polymerisation?","o":["CH₃CH₂Cl","CH₃CH₃","CH₂=CHCl","C₂H₅OH"],"a":2,"x":"Addition polymers form from monomers with a C=C double bond. CH₂=CHCl (chloroethene) is the monomer for poly(chloroethene).","w":["Saturated, with no C=C double bond, so it cannot add on to form a polymer.","Ethane is saturated; no double bond means no addition polymerisation.","","Ethanol has no C=C bond; it is an alcohol, not an addition monomer."]}
{"id":"gch-032","s":"chemistry","t":"4.7.3.4","lv":1,"q":"DNA is a polymer. What are its monomers called?","o":["Amino acids","Glucose units","Nucleotides","Fatty acids"],"a":2,"x":"DNA is made of nucleotides, each with one of four bases. Amino acids make proteins and glucose units make starch and cellulose.","w":["Amino acids are the monomers of proteins, not DNA.","Glucose units make starch and cellulose, not DNA.","","Fatty acids join with glycerol to make lipids, not DNA."]}
{"id":"gch-033","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A pure compound melts at 122 °C. A sample melts over the range 114–119 °C. What does this suggest?","o":["The sample is pure","The sample is a different compound with a sharper melting point","The sample is impure","The thermometer must be broken"],"a":2,"x":"Pure substances have a sharp melting point. Impurities lower the melting point and make it melt over a range of temperatures.","w":["Pure compounds melt sharply at one temperature, not over a range.","","","No need to blame the thermometer; a lower, wider range is the sign of impurities."]}
{"id":"gch-034","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.1.3","lv":2,"q":"In chromatography a spot travels 3.2 cm and the solvent front travels 8.0 cm. What is the Rf value?","o":["2.5","0.40","0.25","4.8"],"a":1,"x":"Rf = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent = 3.2 ÷ 8.0 = 0.40. It has no units and is never more than 1.","w":["Divided solvent by spot (8.0 ÷ 3.2); Rf is spot distance ÷ solvent distance.","","Slip in the division: 3.2 ÷ 8.0 is 0.40, not 0.25.","Subtracted the distances (8.0 − 3.2) instead of dividing."]}
{"id":"gch-035","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.2","lv":1,"q":"A gas bleaches damp litmus paper. Which gas is it?","o":["Hydrogen","Oxygen","Chlorine","Carbon dioxide"],"a":2,"x":"Chlorine turns damp blue litmus red and then bleaches it white. Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop and carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy.","w":["Hydrogen gives a squeaky pop with a lit splint; it does not bleach litmus.","Oxygen relights a glowing splint; it does not bleach litmus.","","Carbon dioxide turns limewater cloudy; it does not bleach litmus."]}
{"id":"gch-036","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.1","lv":3,"q":"A white solid gives a lilac flame. It dissolves, and barium chloride with dilute hydrochloric acid gives a white precipitate. What is the solid?","o":["Potassium sulfate","Potassium chloride","Sodium sulfate","Potassium carbonate"],"a":0,"x":"A lilac flame shows K⁺. A white precipitate with barium chloride and acid shows sulfate. Sodium gives a yellow flame, and carbonates fizz with acid.","w":["","Chloride is tested with silver nitrate; the barium chloride test shows sulfate.","Sodium burns yellow, not lilac; lilac means potassium.","A carbonate would fizz with acid, and the barium test shows sulfate."]}
{"id":"gch-037","s":"chemistry","t":"4.8.3.6","lv":1,"q":"Which is an advantage of instrumental methods over chemical tests?","o":["They are rapid, accurate and sensitive","They are always cheaper","They need no trained operator","They give a result by colour change"],"a":0,"x":"Instrumental methods are quick, accurate and can detect tiny samples. However, the equipment is expensive and needs trained operators.","w":["","Equipment is usually costly to buy and maintain, so not always cheaper.","Instruments need trained operators, so this is the opposite of true.","Colour change is a feature of simple chemical tests, not instruments."]}
{"id":"gch-038","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.1","lv":1,"q":"Which process caused the amount of oxygen in the atmosphere to increase over billions of years?","o":["Gases given out by volcanoes","Photosynthesis by algae and plants","Burning of fossil fuels by humans","Condensation of water vapour into oceans"],"a":1,"x":"Algae and later plants used carbon dioxide for photosynthesis and released oxygen, so oxygen rose and carbon dioxide fell.","w":["Volcanoes mainly released CO₂, water vapour and nitrogen, not much oxygen.","","Fossil fuel burning uses up oxygen and began only recently, not over billions of years.","Condensation formed the oceans, but it does not produce oxygen."]}
{"id":"gch-039","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.2.1","lv":2,"q":"How do greenhouse gases keep the Earth warm?","o":["They absorb infrared radiation from the Earth and re-radiate it","They reflect ultraviolet radiation away from the Earth","They trap heat by stopping the Sun's rays reaching the surface","They absorb all the light from the Sun"],"a":0,"x":"Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide and methane absorb long-wavelength infrared radiation emitted from the Earth and radiate some of it back.","w":["","Ozone deals with UV; greenhouse gases absorb infrared given out by the Earth.","Greenhouse gases let sunlight through; they trap heat leaving the Earth.","They do not absorb all sunlight; most reaches the surface."]}
{"id":"gch-040","s":"chemistry","t":"4.9.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is carbon monoxide poisonous?","o":["It stops the blood carrying oxygen","It causes acid rain","It fills the lungs with soot","It reacts with carbon dioxide in the blood"],"a":0,"x":"Carbon monoxide is colourless and odourless. It combines with haemoglobin and prevents red blood cells carrying oxygen, and it forms in incomplete combustion.","w":["","Acid rain comes from sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, not carbon monoxide.","Soot is a separate product; CO is a gas that stops blood carrying oxygen.","It binds to haemoglobin; it does not react with CO₂ in the blood."]}
{"id":"gch-041","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.1","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is a finite resource?","o":["Wood from sustainably managed forests","Wind","Crude oil","Sunlight"],"a":2,"x":"Finite resources are used up faster than they form. Crude oil takes millions of years to form. Wood, wind and sunlight are renewable.","w":["Sustainably managed forests are replanted, so wood is renewable.","Wind never runs out, so it is renewable.","","Sunlight is continually supplied, so it is renewable."]}
{"id":"gch-042","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.1.2","lv":1,"q":"Why is chlorine added when drinking water is treated?","o":["To remove insoluble solids","To kill microorganisms","To remove dissolved salts","To make the water taste better"],"a":1,"x":"Sterilising uses chlorine, ozone or ultraviolet light to kill microbes. Filtration removes solids.","w":["Insoluble solids are removed by filtration, not by adding chlorine.","","Dissolved salts are removed by distillation, not by chlorination.","Chlorine is added to sterilise the water, not to improve taste."]}
{"id":"gch-043","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.2","lv":1,"q":"What is one benefit of recycling aluminium instead of extracting it from its ore?","o":["It uses much less energy","It makes a purer metal","It uses more electricity","It gives off more carbon dioxide"],"a":0,"x":"Extracting aluminium needs lots of electricity. Recycling saves energy, conserves ore and reduces waste.","w":["","Recycled aluminium is the same metal; purity is not the main benefit.","Recycling uses less electricity, not more.","Recycling cuts CO₂ emissions because it uses less energy."]}
{"id":"gch-044","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.3","lv":2,"q":"Why is steel harder than pure iron?","o":["Steel contains more iron atoms packed into each layer","Steel has no metallic bonding between its iron atoms","The layers of atoms in steel are more regular than in iron","Carbon atoms distort the layers, so they slide less easily"],"a":3,"x":"In steel the carbon atoms are a different size, so the layers of iron atoms cannot slide over each other as easily as in pure iron.","w":["It is the added carbon atoms that make steel harder, not extra iron atoms.","Steel is still metallic; the bonding is still there.","Pure iron has the regular layers, which slide easily; steel's are less regular.",""]}
{"id":"gch-045","s":"chemistry","t":"4.10.4","lv":3,"q":"The Haber process runs at about 450 °C, not lower. Why, given that the reaction is exothermic?","o":["A lower temperature gives a lower yield and a slower rate","A lower temperature makes the catalyst react with nitrogen","At 450 °C the yield of ammonia is the highest possible","Lower temperatures give more ammonia, but too slowly"],"a":3,"x":"The reaction is exothermic, so a lower temperature favours more ammonia. But the rate would be too slow, so 450 °C is a compromise between rate and yield.","w":["Lower temperature gives a higher yield for an exothermic reaction, not lower.","The catalyst is not used up by reacting with nitrogen.","Yield is actually higher at lower temperatures, so 450 °C is not the maximum.",""]}
{"id":"gch-046","s":"chemistry","t":"4.3.2.1","lv":3,"q":"Calcium carbonate decomposes: CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂. What mass of carbon dioxide forms from 10.0 g of CaCO₃? (Ar: Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)","o":["0.44 g","4.4 g","44 g","10.0 g"],"a":1,"x":"Mr of CaCO₃ = 100, so 10.0 g is 0.100 mol. The ratio is 1 : 1, so 0.100 mol CO₂ forms. Mass = 0.100 × 44 = 4.4 g.","w":["Decimal slip: 0.100 × 44 is 4.4 g, not 0.44 g.","","Forgot to use 0.100 mol: 44 g is the mass of one whole mole of CO₂.","Assumed the product mass equals the reactant mass; CaO has mass too."]}
{"id":"gch-047","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.1.4","lv":3,"q":"In Mg + Cu²⁺ → Mg²⁺ + Cu, which statement is correct?","o":["Magnesium is reduced because it loses electrons","Magnesium is oxidised because it loses electrons","Copper ions are oxidised because they gain electrons","Copper ions are oxidised because they lose electrons"],"a":1,"x":"Oxidation is loss of electrons and reduction is gain. Mg loses two electrons and is oxidised, and Cu²⁺ gains two electrons and is reduced.","w":["Reduction is gain of electrons, so losing them means oxidation.","","Copper ions gain electrons, so they are reduced, not oxidised.","Copper ions gain electrons rather than lose them, so they are reduced."]}
{"id":"gch-048","s":"chemistry","t":"4.4.3.5","lv":3,"q":"Molten lead(II) bromide is electrolysed. Which half equation happens at the cathode?","o":["Pb²⁺ → Pb + 2e⁻","Pb²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Pb","2Br⁻ → Br₂ + 2e⁻","Br₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Br⁻"],"a":1,"x":"Positive lead ions move to the cathode and gain electrons, so they are reduced to lead metal. Bromide ions lose electrons at the anode.","w":["This shows electrons lost, which is oxidation, not what happens at the cathode.","","Bromide ions lose electrons at the anode, not the cathode.","The melt has Br⁻ ions, not Br₂; bromine forms at the anode, not the cathode."]}
{"id":"gch-049","s":"chemistry","t":"4.5.1.3","lv":3,"q":"H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. Bond energies in kJ/mol: H–H 436, Cl–Cl 243, H–Cl 432. What is the overall energy change?","o":["+185 kJ/mol","−679 kJ/mol","−185 kJ/mol","−864 kJ/mol"],"a":2,"x":"Energy to break bonds = 436 + 243 = 679. Energy released making bonds = 2 × 432 = 864. Overall change = 679 − 864 = −185 kJ/mol, so it is exothermic.","w":["Sign flipped: took bonds made minus bonds broken, so exothermic looks endothermic.","Used only bonds broken (436 + 243); you must also subtract the 2 × 432 released.","","Only used energy released making bonds (2 × 432) and ignored the energy to break."]}
{"id":"gch-050","s":"chemistry","t":"4.6.2.4","lv":3,"q":"N₂O₄(g) ⇌ 2NO₂(g) is endothermic in the forward direction. What happens if the temperature is raised?","o":["The equilibrium shifts left, giving more N₂O₄","The equilibrium shifts right, giving more NO₂","The position stays the same, only the rate changes","Both reactions stop until it is cooled"],"a":1,"x":"Raising the temperature favours the endothermic direction, which opposes the change. The forward reaction is endothermic, so more NO₂ forms.","w":["Shifted the wrong way: heat favours the endothermic direction, not the exothermic one.","","Temperature changes the equilibrium position, not just the rate of reaction.","Reactions never stop at equilibrium; both carry on at equal rates."]}
